UP|52|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|As you can see defense if very hard. In the original defense, after winning the @DK, East cashed her spade tricks and led a diamond. That gave declarer his 7th trick because, after winning the @DA, West had to lead a heart, giving declarer a 2nd heart trick.|
PG| ***** 17 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Had I let E/W defend as they were going to, North would have made the contract. However, if you carefully count tricks and signal correctly that will significantly help your defense.|
PG| ***** 18 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|North played the hand well. He can only count 6 sure tricks. Although, he might start playing diamonds right away rather than cashing his winners. |
PG| ***** 19 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|That way, it makes it harder for the opponents to find the heart switch and a diamond may provide the extra trick. Or even a heart if they fail to cash the setting trick after taking their 4 spades and a diamond and a heart. Plus spades might be 4-4. Regardless, if defended properly 1N is always down.|
PG| ***** 20 ***** |


