mb|1D|mb|1S?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I could bid 2@S as a limit+ raise in diamonds, but I don't want to miss a heart fit, although a |CP|1|FP|2|AT|^*Hhttp://www.bridgesights.com/hondobridge/hondoviewer/hondoviewer.php?sf=negativedoubles,Negative Double^*N|CP||FP||at| is a better bid.|
PG| ***** 59 ***** |


mb|D|mb|3S|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|Personally, at this favorable vulnerability, (they are vulnerable but you are not) I would have bid 4@S. That is the best time to be very aggressive. There is no reason to change the bid.|
PG| ***** 60 ***** |


mb|4H|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is unclear North to bid 4@H. It is bit of overbid as I could be weak.|
PG| ***** 61 ***** |


mb|P|mb|P|mb|4S|mb|P|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|mb|P?|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|With a singleton spade and a double fit in hearts and diamonds at this vulnerability I think 5@H is probably best by me. On the other hand, in IMPs, 5@D is safer in case there is a diamond ruff. |
PG| ***** 62 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|I am pretty sure we only have an 8 card fit in hearts because partner did not open 1@H. On most auctions where partner opens 1 of a minor and bids a major later when they only have 4 cards in the major because with 5 cards they open the major. |
PG| ***** 63 ***** |


nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|The only exception is when they are 5-6, but that is very rare. BTW, North has not promised diamonds. But if I bid 5@D North can always correct to 5@H.|
PG| ***** 64 ***** |


mb|5D|mb|P?|nt|^z@3^b@2^^|cp|8|AT|^B@2^*Bhondo717: ^*N|cp||at|It is not clear whether to pass 5@D or bid 5@H. Bidding 5@H is a reasonable choice.|
PG| ***** 65 ***** |


